 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE PONY TRACK. Ben Nevis, Beinn Nibheis in Gaelic, stands 1,345 m: the highest mountain in the whole of the British Isles. A weather observatory stood on the summit from 1883 to 1904, with staff living up there all year. Visibility was often nil. The tea, apparently, was good."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "LOCH LINNHE. A long sea loch running from the open Atlantic almost to the foot of Ben Nevis, with seals, otters and the odd porpoise. The town stands where it ends. The Gaelic name for Fort William is An Gearasdan: 'the Garrison'."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE HIGH STREET. Pedestrians only, with pubs, outdoor shops and a hundred ways to buy a mug with a stag on it. It was once next to a fort, named for King William III, long gone. The street's still here. The mugs have multiplied."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE WEST HIGHLAND LINE. Opened in 1894, it runs through moors, lochs and mountains to Mallaig. The famous Jacobite steam train, the one that gets filmed on the Glenfinnan Viaduct, starts right here. The ticket office does not, sadly, do dragons."
  }
 ],
